  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Which of the following lines is perpendicular to $y=x+\\frac{1}{2}?$",
  "worked_solution": "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nThe line $y=x+\\frac{1}{2}$ is in slope-intercept form, and its slope is $1.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y=-x+9$ has a slope of $-1.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n1\\cdot (-1)=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y=-x+9$ is perpendicular to the given line.",
